// Sign in with the OAuth 2.0 authorization code and PKCE, then call an API
時系列図 Sign in with OAuth 2.0 and call an API (authorization code and PKCE)

browser = User's browser「operated by the user」 [色 slate] [印 globe]
app = App「asks for sign-in」 [色 blue] [印 app]
auth = Authorization server「checks who you are」 [色 violet] [印 shield]
API = API「the user's data」 [色 green] [印 server]
帯 Values the app holds = state, code_verifier, authorization code, access token, refresh token

# Press sign in
> The user presses the app's sign-in button. The app never receives an ID or a password and leaves identity checks to the authorization server.
`GET /login`
browser -> app : GET /login

# Make the key material
> The app makes a one-time code_verifier and uses its SHA-256 as the code_challenge. It also makes a state to recognise the return trip and keeps it.
`code_challenge = BASE64URL(SHA-256(code_verifier))`
app : make the key material
帯 code_verifier = dBjf…
帯 state = af0i…

# Send to the authorization server
> The app redirects the browser to the authorization server. code_challenge and state ride in the URL and travel through the browser.
`GET /authorize?code_challenge=…&state=…`
app -> browser : 302 redirect [札 challenge]
browser -> auth : GET /authorize [札 challenge]

# Sign in and consent
> The authorization server checks the user and asks for consent to the scope the app will get. The password never reaches the app.
auth -> browser : sign-in screen
browser -> auth : ID, password and consent

# Return with an authorization code
> The authorization server issues a short-lived authorization code and sends the browser back to the registered redirect_uri. The code also passes through the browser.
`302 redirect_uri?code=…&state=…`
auth -> browser : 302 redirect [札 code]
browser -> app : GET /callback [札 code]
帯 authorization code = Splx…

# Check the state
> The app checks that the returned state equals the one it sent. If not, it treats the return as forged and drops it.
app : compare the state
帯 state [match]

# Exchange the code
> The app sends the authorization code and code_verifier straight to the authorization server, not through the browser.
`POST /token  code=…&code_verifier=…`
app -> auth : direct POST /token [札 code + verifier]
帯 code_verifier [sent]
帯 authorization code [sent]

# Compare
> The authorization server checks that the SHA-256 of code_verifier equals the first code_challenge and that the code is unused and unexpired, then marks the code as used.
auth : compare
帯 authorization code [spent]

# Return the tokens
> If everything matches, it returns an access token and a refresh token. Someone who stole only the code does not know code_verifier, so the previous step stops them.
`access_token, refresh_token, expires_in`
auth -> app : direct 200 tokens [札 access + refresh]
app「holds the tokens」
帯 access token = eyJh… [60 min]
帯 refresh token = 8xLO… [stored]

# Call the API
> The app puts the access token in the Authorization header and calls the API.
`Authorization: Bearer <access_token>`
app -> API : GET /me [札 Bearer]

# Check and answer
> The API checks the token's signature, expiry and allowed scope, and returns the user's data if it passes.
API : check the token
API -> app : 200 data
API「returned the data」

# Return the signed-in page
> The app builds a page from the data and returns it. When the access token expires, it gets a new one with the refresh token.
app -> browser : signed-in page
browser「signed in」
